{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1067","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1067","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1067\",\"parent_revision\":\"f01e5c1d26e96dc27da74a3688b8fa7cf3202a73\",\"root_revision\":\"b083cbe3080a47cac9cbf5c39c224f45b0e21951c446b54de778b620dea81d8a\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe `MapSystems` step in `.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md` identifies candidate callers by scanning individual agents (`.claude/agents/`), individual skills (`.claude/skills/`), hooks, and workflow scripts. It never evaluates whether the researched tool addresses the same problem domain as an **existing workflow as a whole**.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAny researched tool that is an architectural alternative to an existing workflow (rather than a drop-in dependency for a single agent) will be silently dismissed as `no_utilization_surface`. The assessment produces false negatives for the highest-value integration opportunities \u2014 cases where a tool could replace or substantially improve an entire workflow, not just augment one agent.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-08)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n7/10 \u2014 This is the highest-value integration category (tool replaces a weaker workflow) and it is systematically invisible to the assessor. Every future research entry that is an architectural alternative will silently return no proposals. The bug produces false negatives on the most impactful opportunities.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Bottleneck: all future research entries for workflow-class tools (knowledge bases, orchestrators, indexing services) will be silently dismissed\\\\n- The research-curator workflow is the primary affected workflow \u2014 any tool that could replace its markdown+Grep/Glob architecture is invisible to the assessor\\\\n- False negatives in utilization assessment mean high-value integration opportunities are never surfaced\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen the `MapSystems` step runs, before iterating over individual agent and skill files, the agent evaluates whether the researched tool addresses the same problem domain as any existing multi-agent workflow in the repo. If a workflow-level candidate is found, a utilization proposal is written for it. The per-file loop continues afterward for individual-agent candidates.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nAfter this fix:\\\\n- The `MapSystems` step in `.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md` includes a workflow-aggregate check before the `ForEach` node\\\\n- The agent's Mermaid flowchart reflects this new step\\\\n- Running the assessor against a research entry like Samuraizer produces a workflow-level proposal in addition to (or instead of) individual-agent proposals\\\\n- The `Skipped Systems` table in the output file distinguishes \\\"no individual-file overlap\\\" from \\\"workflow-level candidate identified\\\"\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md` contains a workflow-level assessment step between `Extract integration surfaces` and `ForEach candidate local system`\\\\n2. The new step explicitly asks whether the researched tool addresses the same problem domain as an existing workflow (e.g., research-curator, a pipeline, a knowledge base)\\\\n3. Running the assessor against a research entry for Samuraizer (or any equivalent knowledge-base tool) produces at least one proposal targeting the research-curator workflow as a whole\\\\n4. The output file format is unchanged \u2014 proposals use the existing `## Utilization N:` structure; the `caller` field may reference a workflow path rather than a single file\\\\n5. The `Return Format` section of the agent handles the case where a workflow-level proposal was written but no individual-file proposals were found\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\\\n|------|------|\\\\n| Agent | @research-utilization-assessor \u2014 `.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md` (the file to fix) |\\\\n| Agent | @research-curator \u2014 `.claude/agents/research-curator.md` (the primary workflow that is a candidate) |\\\\n| Prior work | `research/insights/2026-03-25-samuraizer-utilization.md` \u2014 manually written correct output; reference for expected proposal format |\\\\n| Prior work | `research/ai-research-tools/samuraizer.md` \u2014 research entry that triggered the false negative |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None\\\\n- Blocks: None identified\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 single agent file edit. The fix is a new workflow step and Mermaid node insertion before the `ForEach` loop. The proposal format is unchanged; only the candidate-identification logic expands. No MCP tools, no schema, no plugin manifest changes.\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: Bug\\\\n\\\\n**Rationale**: The `MapSystems` step has a logic gap \u2014 it only evaluates individual files as callers, never workflow-level patterns. The Mermaid flowchart in the agent file confirms the gap: `MapSystems` lists individual agent/skill/hook/script directories as the complete candidate set with no workflow-aggregate check.\\\\n\\\\n**Impact radius**: Single agent file (`.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md`). No schema changes, no MCP tools, no plugin manifest changes. The fix is a workflow step insertion before the `ForEach` node in the Mermaid flowchart.\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\n- Agent returns `STATUS: no_utilization_surface` for tools that are architectural alternatives to existing workflows\\\\n- Manually written corrected output at `research/insights/2026-03-25-samuraizer-utilization.md` shows what the agent should have produced\\\\n- Gap is observable in the Mermaid flowchart in `.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md`: `MapSystems` node \u2192 `ForEach` node with no intermediate workflow-aggregate check\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\nAPPROVED-FOR-PLANNING. All 4 conditions AVAILABLE: broken step identified in MapSystems (lines 33-35 of research-utilization-assessor.md), false negative documented (Samuraizer 2026-03-25), fix approach stated (add workflow-level check before ForEach), agent file editable.\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Identify any research entry for a tool that is an architectural alternative to an existing multi-agent workflow (e.g., a knowledge base, pipeline orchestrator, file-indexing service)\\\\n2. Invoke `@research-utilization-assessor` against that research entry\\\\n3. Observe the agent evaluates individual `.claude/agents/*.md` and `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` files as candidate callers\\\\n4. Observe no step asks \\\"does this tool replace or substantially improve the research-curator workflow as a whole?\\\"\\\\n5. Observe verdict is `no_utilization_surface` or `PROPOSALS_WRITTEN: 0` despite a genuine workflow-level integration opportunity existing\\\\n\\\\n**Confirmed instance**: Samuraizer on 2026-03-25 \u2014 `research/insights/2026-03-25-samuraizer-utilization.md` is the manually written corrected output.\\n\\n## Where It Was Observed\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen Samuraizer (`research/ai-research-tools/samuraizer.md`) was assessed on 2026-03-25, the agent returned `STATUS: no_utilization_surface`. The agent's reasoning: \\\"existing agents are pure information-gathering agents designed for file-based operations, not external API consumption.\\\"\\n\\nThis conclusion is wrong. The research-curator workflow (curator agent + cross-referencer + README index) is collectively a knowledge base: it ingests URLs, writes markdown entries, and retrieves related entries via Grep/Glob keyword scoring. Samuraizer is a purpose-built knowledge base with vector embeddings, semantic search, and RAG chat \u2014 a stronger implementation of exactly what the research-curator workflow does. The integration surface exists at the **workflow level**, not the individual-agent level.\\n\\nThe corrected utilization proposal was written manually at `research/insights/2026-03-25-samuraizer-utilization.md`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Verbatim User Report\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n> research-utilization-assessor misses workflow-level candidates: the MapSystems step searches for individual agents/skills as callers but never evaluates whether the researched tool is an architectural alternative to the research-curator workflow as a whole. When Samuraizer (a knowledge base with semantic search) was assessed against this repo, the agent concluded no_utilization_surface because individual agents don't make external API calls \u2014 missing that the entire research-curator markdown+Grep/Glob architecture is the weaker implementation Samuraizer would replace. Fix: add a workflow-level assessment step to MapSystems that asks \\\"does this tool address the same problem domain as an existing workflow?\\\" before checking individual agent files. The agent is at .claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md\\n</div>\\n\\n## What Is Broken\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe `MapSystems` step in `.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md` identifies candidate callers by scanning individual agents (`.claude/agents/`), individual skills (`.claude/skills/`), hooks, and workflow scripts. It never evaluates whether the researched tool addresses the same problem domain as an **existing workflow as a whole**.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"88da254459e148d3818e9b261157f4f9e19fe9cc7197aac981bebaf759958c68\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPg6fvA\"}"}